WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.448
This is research from
the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:06.462
Workbook's Research section,
and it's still under review.

4
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:10.926
It answers a question architects hit as
soon as workloads move to the cloud.

5
00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:16.737
If we already run Infoblox Trinzic
appliances on premises, can they handle DNS,

6
00:00:16.737 --> 00:00:22.013
DHCP and address management for AWS,
Azure and Google Cloud on their own?

7
00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:25.050
Or do we need some form of
cloud-based DDI as well?

8
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.643
It's written for someone still learning
DDI, so we'll start with the vocabulary,

9
00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:32.298
look at where Trinzic
stops at the cloud edge,

10
00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:35.025
then weigh three options
and a decision table.

11
00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:40.309
Every fact comes from vendor or government
documentation opened on October

12
00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:45.263
twenty-second, 2026, and recommendations
are labeled as design guidance.

13
00:00:45.263 --> 00:00:47.170
Let's start with the words.

14
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.433
A few words first, because the
rest of the deck leans on them.

15
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:59.507
DDI means DNS, DHCP and IPAM:
names, addresses handed out,

16
00:00:59.507 --> 00:01:03.719
and the record of who owns
which address ranges.

17
00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:08.541
An Infoblox Grid is a set of appliances
sharing one database, run from the

18
00:01:08.541 --> 00:01:13.120
Grid Master, which is also the only
one that can sign zones with DNSSEC.

19
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:19.414
Trinzic is the physical appliance, vNIOS
is the same software as a virtual machine,

20
00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:22.932
and Universal DDI is
Infoblox's cloud service.

21
00:01:22.932 --> 00:01:25.058
On the cloud side, think resolvers,

22
00:01:25.058 --> 00:01:29.007
endpoints and private zones that
only linked networks can see.

23
00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:31.395
And the word to remember is split-brain:

24
00:01:31.395 --> 00:01:35.870
two systems each believing they own
a name, and giving different answers.

25
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.995
Avoiding it is the point of this deck.

26
00:01:37.995 --> 00:01:39.960
So what does Trinzic do well?

27
00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.556
On premises, a Trinzic Grid is
the agency's DNS and DHCP servers

28
00:01:48.556 --> 00:01:53.022
plus its address record, all
managed from the Grid Master.

29
00:01:53.022 --> 00:01:54.261
It does a lot well.

30
00:01:54.261 --> 00:02:00.100
DNS views give internal and outside
clients different answers on purpose, DNS

31
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.412
Firewall blocks bad names,
DNSSEC signing is centralized,

32
00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:06.748
and DHCP runs as failover pairs.

33
00:02:06.748 --> 00:02:11.114
One caution: the default signing
algorithm is RSA with SHA-1,

34
00:02:11.114 --> 00:02:14.234
which a federal deployment should change.

35
00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:19.851
This matters because Book 08 of the
workbook argues for one DDI policy plane,

36
00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:24.397
a single system of record with
cloud DNS managed as an overlay.

37
00:02:24.397 --> 00:02:26.749
On premises, the Grid fits that role,

38
00:02:26.749 --> 00:02:31.535
inside the agency's own boundary rather
than as a FedRAMP cloud service.

39
00:02:31.535 --> 00:02:34.290
The open question is the cloud edge.

40
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.202
At the cloud edge, Trinzic alone
can see, but it can't control.

41
00:02:42.202 --> 00:02:46.167
It can discover cloud networks and
virtual machines on a schedule,

42
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.690
including GovCloud and Azure Government,

43
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:53.015
and it can import cloud zones so
on-premises clients resolve cloud names.

44
00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:57.377
But that import is one way, and
edits made in NIOS get overwritten.

45
00:02:57.377 --> 00:03:02.466
It can't create or own a cloud private
zone, manage cloud resolver rules,

46
00:03:02.466 --> 00:03:05.888
act as the outside
authority for AWS VPC IPAM,

47
00:03:05.888 --> 00:03:10.889
or run DHCP for cloud subnets; the
clouds hand out their own addresses.

48
00:03:10.889 --> 00:03:15.582
So, as an inference: in the cloud,
Infoblox's job is DNS and IPAM,

49
00:03:15.582 --> 00:03:20.365
and the cloud's own resolver
still answers cloud private zones.

50
00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:22.570
That leads to three options.

51
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.702
Option A puts vNIOS
members inside each cloud,

52
00:03:29.702 --> 00:03:35.308
as Grid members in high-availability
pairs, usually in a shared-services hub.

53
00:03:35.308 --> 00:03:39.269
Signed zones stay signed, with
the keys kept on the Grid Master.

54
00:03:39.269 --> 00:03:42.595
Workloads reach vNIOS in one of two ways.

55
00:03:42.595 --> 00:03:47.635
Either you point the network's DNS
straight at vNIOS, which is simple,

56
00:03:47.635 --> 00:03:53.395
but in Azure it means vNIOS must forward
Azure-private names back to Azure DNS.

57
00:03:53.395 --> 00:03:56.440
Or you keep the cloud's
own resolver and forward

58
00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.720
chosen domains to vNIOS,
so clients change nothing.

59
00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.952
One AWS caution: to resolve Route 53
private zones for other networks,

60
00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:09.908
forward to an inbound endpoint,
not the built-in resolver address.

61
00:04:09.908 --> 00:04:12.848
And every vNIOS member is agency-operated,

62
00:04:12.848 --> 00:04:15.871
with no FedRAMP authorization covering it.

63
00:04:15.871 --> 00:04:18.170
Next, the cloud service.

64
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:27.411
Option B joins the Grid to Universal
DDI, Infoblox's software-as-a-service

65
00:04:27.411 --> 00:04:31.895
control plane, while the Grid
stays primary for its DNS data.

66
00:04:31.895 --> 00:04:34.836
What it adds is the one
thing the others lack:

67
00:04:34.836 --> 00:04:39.820
it's the only documented Infoblox
mechanism that writes back to cloud DNS.

68
00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:44.617
It syncs Route 53, Azure DNS
and Google Cloud DNS both ways,

69
00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:50.332
and on commercial AWS it can be
the outside authority for VPC IPAM.

70
00:04:50.332 --> 00:04:51.982
The limits are real, though.

71
00:04:51.982 --> 00:04:56.310
DNSSEC records aren't synced, some
zones can't be created from the portal,

72
00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:59.171
and the Grid Master stays
the recovery console.

73
00:04:59.171 --> 00:05:02.648
The government edition is
FedRAMP Certified at Moderate,

74
00:05:02.648 --> 00:05:06.995
but with narrower cloud discovery, so
confirm the details with Infoblox.

75
00:05:06.995 --> 00:05:08.800
Now the simplest option.

76
00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.476
Option C keeps the Grid
authoritative for agency zones

77
00:05:16.476 --> 00:05:20.488
and lets each cloud's native
DNS own its own private zones.

78
00:05:20.488 --> 00:05:24.645
The two meet through conditional
forwarding: a rule that says,

79
00:05:24.645 --> 00:05:27.825
for names ending in this
domain, ask that server.

80
00:05:27.825 --> 00:05:31.847
The Grid forwards cloud domains
inward to the cloud's endpoints,

81
00:05:31.847 --> 00:05:35.338
and each cloud forwards agency
domains back to the Grid.

82
00:05:35.338 --> 00:05:37.762
Google recommends exactly this design.

83
00:05:37.762 --> 00:05:41.427
One fact to keep in mind: a
Route 53 private zone asked from

84
00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:45.950
outside its linked networks resolves
on the public internet instead.

85
00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.875
Addresses follow the same idea.

86
00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:52.197
The Grid owns the agency's blocks
and hands each cloud a block,

87
00:05:52.197 --> 00:05:57.125
automated on commercial AWS and
manual in GovCloud, Azure and Google.

88
00:05:57.125 --> 00:05:59.790
Whichever you pick, some rules apply.

89
00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.078
Whatever option you pick, four
rules keep split-brain away.

90
00:06:07.078 --> 00:06:11.073
First, every zone has exactly
one writable owner, never two,

91
00:06:11.073 --> 00:06:15.152
and everything else forwards to
it or holds a read-only copy.

92
00:06:15.152 --> 00:06:19.402
Decide ownership before you turn
sync on, because sync overwrites.

93
00:06:19.402 --> 00:06:22.165
Second, divide the namespace by owner.

94
00:06:22.165 --> 00:06:26.193
For example, corp dot agency
dot gov lives on the Grid,

95
00:06:26.193 --> 00:06:30.128
and aws dot corp dot agency
dot gov lives in Route 53.

96
00:06:30.128 --> 00:06:35.114
Third, DNSSEC keys stay on the Grid
Master or its hardware security module,

97
00:06:35.114 --> 00:06:38.139
and validation belongs
on agency resolvers.

98
00:06:38.139 --> 00:06:40.897
Fourth, address authority
stays on the Grid,

99
00:06:40.897 --> 00:06:44.802
with clouds getting delegated
blocks so overlaps get caught.

100
00:06:44.802 --> 00:06:47.830
With those rules in place,
here's how to choose.

101
00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.805
Here's the decision table, and every row
is design guidance, not an endorsement.

102
00:06:56.805 --> 00:07:00.935
If you're mostly on premises with a
small cloud footprint, Trinzic plus

103
00:07:00.935 --> 00:07:05.493
native cloud resolvers and conditional
forwarding is the lowest-cost start.

104
00:07:05.493 --> 00:07:08.830
Hybrid with one cloud,
add a vNIOS pair in a hub

105
00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:12.618
network so resolution stays
local if the WAN fails.

106
00:07:12.618 --> 00:07:15.406
Multi-cloud at FedRAMP Moderate and below,

107
00:07:15.406 --> 00:07:18.354
add Universal DDI
through Government Cloud.

108
00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:24.043
And for GovCloud, Azure Government, FedRAMP
High or Defense Impact Levels 4 and 5,

109
00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:28.955
stay with options A and C, because
Infoblox's service is Moderate-only.

110
00:07:28.955 --> 00:07:32.963
In short: Trinzic plus native
resolvers as the baseline,

111
00:07:32.963 --> 00:07:38.200
with vNIOS and Universal DDI added
only where they earn their place.

112
00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.850
To close, where this fits.

113
00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:48.844
Book 08 of the workbook makes the
vendor-neutral case, from split-brain to the

114
00:07:48.844 --> 00:07:54.313
policy plane, and the research deep dive on
Infoblox DDI covers the platform itself.

115
00:07:54.313 --> 00:07:59.317
The decisions to take are which option
for each cloud and impact level, one named

116
00:07:59.317 --> 00:08:02.047
owner for every zone,
where DNSSEC keys live,

117
00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:04.550
and how address blocks reach each cloud.

118
00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.956
The next steps start with
Infoblox: ask for the Government

119
00:08:07.956 --> 00:08:11.575
Cloud boundary and a feature
matrix for government accounts.

120
00:08:11.575 --> 00:08:15.946
Then confirm Azure Government
availability, size NIOS-X capacity if

121
00:08:15.946 --> 00:08:20.163
you want cloud forwarders, and test
forwarding in both directions.

122
00:08:20.163 --> 00:08:25.088
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

123
00:08:25.088 --> 00:08:25.920
Thank you.
